-- right-of-use-assets-reading-a-fall
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>A right-of-use asset is a lessee's right to use a leased asset, measured from the present value of the lease payments and depreciated straight-line. Whether a rig contract creates one depends on who directs the rig.</p></li><li><p>The line falls every year through depreciation. In the case below, a three-year and a five-year lease lose 45.7 percent of their carrying amount in their second year with no event at all.</p></li><li><p>A fall has to be split into depreciation, terminations and extensions using the asset rollforward, the lease liability and the maturity table. In the case, the three combine into a 39.0 percent fall.</p></li></ul><hr><h2>The reported line fell 39 percent, and a fall that size can come from depreciation alone</h2><p>The balance sheet on this page reports right-of-use assets, net, of 123,977 at 31 December 2024 against 203,379 a year earlier, in thousands. That is a fall of 79,402, or 39.0 percent, taking the line from 0.65 to 0.41 percent of total assets.</p><p>This essay uses PT Hulu Energi Nusantara ("HEN"), an upstream oil and gas contractor operating under production sharing contracts (PSCs) with SKK Migas, reporting in USD under Indonesian SAK with a 31 December year end. HEN and every figure attached to it are constructed for illustration. HEN amounts are in USD thousand; day rates are in USD thousand per day.</p><p>HEN operates its block with a 60 percent participating interest, its share of the block's costs and production; partners hold the other 40 percent, and the PSC runs to 2040 (constructed). On 1 January 2023 HEN leased a jack-up rig (an offshore drilling rig that stands on legs lowered to the seabed) for three years and an onshore warehouse for five. In February 2025 the audit committee chair, reading the draft 2024 notes, sees right-of-use assets down from 37,801.0 to 23,051.7, a fall of 39.0 percent, and asks whether the rig was released early or the leases are running down.</p><p>The governing claim of this essay is that a change in right-of-use assets nets depreciation, which runs every year, against term events, which only the lease liability and the maturity table (the note listing undiscounted lease payments by due date) reveal, so the percentage alone cannot say which happened.</p><p>This is a mechanism essay. Its verdict is that HEN's 39.0 percent fall nets a 45.7 percent fall from depreciation, a rig release and a warehouse extension, a split any reader can rebuild from the notes.</p><p>The governing standard is PSAK 116 <em>Leases</em> (formerly PSAK 73, IFRS 16), effective in Indonesia since 2020 under its old number. It replaced PSAK 30 (IAS 17), which kept operating leases off the lessee's balance sheet.</p><h2>A rig contract creates a right-of-use asset only if HEN directs the rig</h2><p>A contract contains a lease when it conveys the right to control the use of an identified asset for a period in exchange for consideration (PSAK 116 paragraph 9), which means the right to substantially all of its economic benefits and the right to direct how and for what purpose it is used. Otherwise it is a service, and nothing reaches this line.</p><p>HEN's contract names one jack-up, and the owner may substitute another only if that rig is damaged. A right that applies only when the asset is not working does not stop it being identified (paragraph B18). A general right would also fail, because a substitution right counts only if the supplier could practically substitute and would gain from doing so (paragraph B14), and moving a jack-up costs its owner several million USD (in whole dollars). The asset is identified.</p><p>HEN decides which wells the rig drills, in what order, to what depth and when it moves. Those are the decisions about how and for what purpose the rig is used (paragraph B24). The owner's crew operates the rig on HEN's instructions, which gives the owner no such right. HEN has a lease. Had a drilling company undertaken to drill five wells with any rig from its fleet, choosing which rig and when, HEN would be buying a service.</p><p>The exclusion of leases to explore for or use oil and gas (paragraph 3(a)) covers the PSC's right to the resource, not the rig or the warehouse.</p><p>The day rate also pays for services. HEN pays USD 80 thousand a day for the rig, crew, maintenance and catering. Rigs of this class charter bareboat, the rig alone with the charterer supplying the crew, for about USD 48 thousand a day, and the services are priced at USD 32 thousand. PSAK 116 allocates the price on relative stand-alone prices (paragraph 13).</p><blockquote><p>Lease component = 80 × 48 / (48 + 32) = USD 48 thousand per day</p><p>Annual lease payment = 48 × 365 = 17,520; annual service cost = 32 × 365 = 11,680, recognised as the services are received</p></blockquote><p>A lessee may elect, by class of asset, not to separate the components (paragraph 15). The liability would then capitalise 29,200 a year, 76,630.0 instead of the 45,978.0 computed below, 67 percent more.</p><h2>The liability is the present value of the payments, and the asset adds what HEN spent to get the assets into use</h2><p>The lease liability at commencement is the present value of the unpaid lease payments, discounted at the rate implicit in the lease or, if that cannot be readily determined, at the lessee's incremental borrowing rate, what it would pay to borrow over a similar term against similar security (paragraph 26). HEN uses 7 percent. Both leases pay annually in arrears, simplifying monthly invoicing, and the annuity factor (the present value of 1 a year for n years) is (1 − 1.07^−n) / 0.07.</p><blockquote><p>Rig liability = 17,520 × (1 − 1.07^−3) / 0.07 = 17,520 × 2.624316 = 45,978.0</p><p>Warehouse liability = 1,925 × (1 − 1.07^−5) / 0.07 = 1,925 × 4.100197 = 7,892.9</p></blockquote><p>The right-of-use asset is the liability plus lease payments made at or before commencement, initial direct costs and the estimated cost of restoring the site that the lease obliges the lessee to bear (paragraph 24). HEN paid the rig owner a mobilisation fee of 1,000 in December 2022 to tow the rig to location. HEN treats it as a payment for making the leased rig available and puts it all in the lease component; a 48/32 allocation would put 600 there. HEN paid an agent's commission of 60 on the warehouse, and must restore the floor slab at exit at an estimated 210.4 in 2027, 150.0 discounted at 7 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Rig asset = 45,978.0 + 1,000 = 46,978.0</p><p>Warehouse asset = 7,892.9 + 60 + 150.0 = 8,102.9</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Right-of-use assets 55,080.9<br>Cr Lease liabilities 53,870.9<br>Cr Prepaid mobilisation 1,000.0<br>Cr Cash (agent's commission) 60.0<br>Cr Restoration provision 150.0</p></blockquote><h2>Straight-line depreciation plus effective interest front-loads the cost of a lease</h2><p>The rig costs HEN 18,877.8 in its first year against a payment of 17,520, because interest is charged on the opening liability at the discount rate (the effective interest method) and is largest when the liability is largest, while depreciation is straight-line over the shorter of the lease term and the asset's life.</p><table style="min-width: 175px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Opening liability</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest at 7%</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Payment</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Closing liability</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Depreciation</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest plus depreciation</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>45,978.0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,218.5</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>17,520</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31,676.5</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>15,659.3</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>18,877.8</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31,676.5</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,217.4</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>17,520</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>16,373.8</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>15,659.3</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>17,876.7</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>16,373.8</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,146.2</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>17,520</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0.0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>15,659.4</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>16,805.6</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Depreciation = 46,978.0 / 3 = 15,659.3 a year, with 0.1 of rounding in 2025</p><p>Total charge = 46,978.0 + 3,218.5 + 2,217.4 + 1,146.2 = 53,560.1, the 52,560 of payments plus the 1,000 fee, with 0.1 of rounding</p></blockquote><p>Under PSAK 30 the rig would have cost 17,520 + 1,000 / 3 = 17,853.3 a year; PSAK 116 charges 1,024.5 more in 2023 and 1,047.7 less in 2025. Where the rig drills development wells, the depreciation and the services are capitalised into oil and gas properties and reach profit through depletion.</p><p>The warehouse depreciates at 8,102.9 / 5 = 1,620.6 a year. At 31 December 2023 the line holds 31,318.7 for the rig and 6,482.3 for the warehouse, 37,801.0 in total.</p><p>The asset is non-current in full. The current/non-current boundary runs through the liability, whose current part is the principal due within twelve months. At 31 December 2023 that is 15,302.7 for the rig (31,676.5 − 16,373.8) and 1,468.6 for the warehouse (6,520.4 − 5,051.8), 16,771.3 in total, leaving 38,196.9 − 16,771.3 = 21,425.6 non-current.</p><h2>Short-term and low-value leases never reach the line</h2><p>A lessee may expense two kinds of lease straight-line instead (paragraph 5). A short-term lease runs 12 months or less from commencement with no purchase option, elected by class of asset. A low-value lease, judged lease by lease, is one whose asset is worth about USD 5,000 (in whole dollars) or less when new, such as a laptop. In 2024 HEN expensed a 75-day liftboat (a self-elevating service vessel) hire at USD 12 thousand a day, 900 in total.</p><h2>The lease term is a judgment, and it moves the line in both directions</h2><p>Two board decisions on 31 December 2024 moved HEN's leases in opposite directions. The lease term is the non-cancellable period plus option periods the lessee is reasonably certain, a threshold well above "more likely than not", to extend into or not to terminate. The warehouse carried a ten-year extension option at the same rent. At commencement HEN had not decided where to hold production spares after drilling, and a shared base with a neighbouring operator was an option, so the extension was not reasonably certain.</p><p>The term is reassessed when a significant event within the lessee's control changes that certainty (paragraph 20), such as a business decision or significant leasehold improvements (paragraph B41). In 2024 an appraisal well, drilled to measure a discovery, came up dry. That ended drilling, but it was outside HEN's control and says nothing about storage. On 31 December 2024 the board approved a production plan that makes the warehouse the field's spares hub until 2037, within the PSC's term. By that date HEN had bolted 1,800 of heavy racking to the slab; leaving early would forfeit most of it and cost about 250 to remove. The decision and the racking are within HEN's control, as paragraph 20 requires, and make the extension reasonably certain. The liability is remeasured over thirteen remaining payments at the incremental borrowing rate at that date, 8 percent for the tenor, and the asset moves by the same amount (paragraphs 39–40).</p><blockquote><p>Revised warehouse liability = 1,925 × (1 − 1.08^−13) / 0.08 = 1,925 × 7.903776 = 15,214.8</p><p>Increase = 15,214.8 − 5,051.8 = 10,163.0</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Right-of-use assets 10,163.0 / Cr Lease liabilities 10,163.0</p></blockquote><p>After two years of unwinding at 7 percent, charged as finance cost, the restoration provision stood at 210.4 / 1.07^3 = 171.7; due in 2037 it is 210.4 / 1.07^13 = 87.3, and under ISAK 101 (IFRIC 1) the 84.4 change reduces the asset.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Restoration provision 84.4 / Cr Right-of-use assets 84.4</p></blockquote><p>The board's other decision cut the drilling programme after the dry well. The rig contract had no early-termination option, but on 31 December 2024 the owner agreed to an early release (a partial termination) on 30 June 2025, six months early and without a fee, because another client was waiting. A change agreed after commencement and outside the original terms is a lease modification, and one that decreases scope is accounted for in two steps (paragraph 46(a)). First, asset and liability are cut in proportion to the scope removed, the difference going to profit or loss. Second, the liability is remeasured at a revised rate (HEN's six-month rate, 7 percent), and the asset moves by that amount. HEN measures scope by remaining term, a judgment, so it removes half.</p><blockquote><p>Rig asset before the modification = 31,318.7 − 15,659.3 = 15,659.4; rig liability = 16,373.8</p><p>Step 1: asset reduced by 15,659.4 × 50% = 7,829.7; liability reduced by 16,373.8 × 50% = 8,186.9</p><p>Gain on modification = 8,186.9 − 7,829.7 = 357.2</p><p>Step 2: revised liability = 8,760 due 30 June 2025 / 1.07^0.5 = 8,468.6; adjustment = 8,468.6 − (16,373.8 − 8,186.9) = 281.7</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Lease liabilities 8,186.9 / Cr Right-of-use assets 7,829.7 / Cr Gain on lease modification 357.2</p><p>Dr Right-of-use assets 281.7 / Cr Lease liabilities 281.7</p></blockquote><p>The rig's carrying amount at 31 December 2024 is 15,659.4 − 7,829.7 + 281.7 = 8,111.4. Had a termination right been in the original contract, becoming reasonably certain to use it would have been a reassessment, with the whole change taken to the asset and no gain.</p><h2>The rollforward, the liability and the maturity table separate depreciation from term events</h2><p>HEN's line fell 14,749.3 in 2024, less than its depreciation of 17,279.9, because the term events added a net 2,530.6.</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Rig</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Warehouse</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Total</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1 January</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31,318.7</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,482.3</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>37,801.0</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Depreciation</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(15,659.3)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(1,620.6)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(17,279.9)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Modification, step 1</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(7,829.7)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(7,829.7)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Modification, step 2</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>281.7</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>281.7</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Extension reassessment</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10,163.0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10,163.0</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Restoration estimate</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(84.4)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(84.4)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 December</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>8,111.4</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>14,940.3</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>23,051.7</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Depreciation alone = 37,801.0 − 17,279.9 = 20,521.1, a fall of 45.7 percent</p><p>Adding the rig modification = 20,521.1 − 7,829.7 + 281.7 = 12,973.1, a fall of 65.7 percent</p><p>Adding the warehouse events = 12,973.1 + 10,163.0 − 84.4 = 23,051.7, a fall of 39.0 percent</p></blockquote><p>The liability gives a second test, because with no term events it falls by the principal repaid. HEN paid 19,445 in 2024 against interest of 2,217.4 + 456.4 = 2,673.8, repaying 16,771.2. The liability fell from 38,196.9 to 23,683.4 (8,468.6 for the rig and 15,214.8 for the warehouse), only 14,513.5. The gap is the rig modification, 8,186.9 − 281.7 = 7,905.2 down, and the extension, 10,163.0 up (0.1 of rounding). A liability that falls more than principal repaid signals terminations; less signals new leases or extensions.</p><p>The maturity table, which PSAK 116 paragraph 58 requires through PSAK 107 (formerly 60, IFRS 7) paragraphs 39 and B11, places the event in time.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Undiscounted lease payments</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Within 1 year</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>19,445</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10,685</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1 to 2 years</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>19,445</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,925</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2 to 5 years</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,850</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5,775</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Over 5 years</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>15,400</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>42,740</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>33,785</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Last year's "1 to 2 years" amount of 19,445 should reappear as this year's "within 1 year". It shows 10,685, so 8,760, six months of rig payments, has gone, a termination. The "over 5 years" amount appears from nothing, an extension or a new long lease. The total is 33,785 against 42,740 − 19,445 = 23,295 with no events, the difference being 10 × 1,925 = 19,250 added less 8,760 removed. The same tests would say whether the reported 39 percent is runoff without renewal or leases ended early.</p><h2>At a PSC operator the line carries the partners' share, and cost recovery runs on a different clock</h2><p>HEN carries 100 percent of both leases on its balance sheet although it bears only 60 percent of their cost, because it signed them in its own name as operator. In March 2019 the IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded that under IFRS 11, now PSAK 111 (formerly 66), a joint operator recognises the liabilities for which it has primary responsibility, including a lease on which it is the primary obligor, the party the lessor can pursue for payment. The partners repay 40 percent of each payment as partner reimbursements billed under the joint operating agreement (JOA), so 40% × 23,051.7 = 9,220.7 of the line is theirs in substance.</p><p>The asset side is less settled, and the Committee left it unaddressed. If the JOA gives the partners a right to use the rig, HEN has a sublease, a lease of the asset onward to them, and moves their share of the asset into a receivable. If HEN alone directs the rig, it keeps the full asset, which is HEN's policy. It then books partner reimbursements as they are billed, in 2024 Dr Receivable from partners 7,778 (40% × 19,445) / Cr Lease costs 7,778. Reimbursements follow the payments while the expense follows depreciation and interest, so HEN's net charge is front-loaded. Non-operating partners book their share through the billings.</p><p>Cost recovery is the PSC mechanism by which the contractor recoups approved operating costs from production before the remainder is split with the government. This essay assumes rentals are recovered as the lease payments fall due, not as depreciation and interest, and that the rig, unlike equipment HEN buys, stays the owner's property. If PSC tax deductions follow the same payments, the asset, the lease liability and (if restoration is deductible when spent) the provision all have a tax base, the amount the tax rules attribute to them, of nil. Since the PSAK 212 (formerly 46, IAS 12) single-transaction amendment, effective 1 January 2023, these differences are recognised gross. On HEN's 60 percent share at the combined PSC rate of 40 percent, that is a deferred tax liability of 5,532.4 on the asset against deferred tax assets of 5,684.0 on the lease liability and 21.0 on the provision, a net asset of 172.6 at 31 December 2024.</p><h2>HEN's line fell 39.0 percent because a warehouse extension offset most of a rig release in a year when depreciation alone would have cut 45.7 percent</h2><p>The chair's answer is that the rig was released early, the leases ran down, and a third event pulled the other way. Depreciation alone would have left 20,521.1; the rig release removed a net 7,548.0 (7,829.7 less 281.7) with a gain of 357.2; the warehouse extension added a net 10,078.6 (10,163.0 less 84.4). Without the rollforward, the modification gain and the maturity table in the notes, HEN's 39.0 percent reads exactly like the reported company's.</p><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>Cost recovery and PSC tax following lease payments and restoration spend, and the rig remaining the lessor's property, were not checked against a PSC, PP 79/2010 as amended or SKK Migas rules. If deductions followed the accounting charge, the deferred tax would be nil.</p></li><li><p>The asset-side treatment in the joint operation (no sublease, partner reimbursements credited to lease costs) is a constructed policy. Deferred tax on the partners' 40 percent carried gross is left out.</p></li><li><p>Putting the whole mobilisation fee in the lease component is a judgment; an auditor could require the 48/32 allocation, which would make the rig asset 46,578.0.</p></li><li><p>Measuring a term reduction by remaining term in step 1 is one reading; the standard's illustrative example reduces floor space, not term.</p></li><li><p>The PSAK 116 paragraph numbers follow IFRS 16 and were not checked against the Indonesian text; real rig contracts add standby and moving rates that complicate the 48/32 allocation.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PSAK 116 <em>Sewa</em> (formerly PSAK 73; IFRS 16 <em>Leases</em>), paragraphs 3, 5, 9, 13, 15, 20, 24, 26, 39–40, 46, 58, B14, B18, B24, B41. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. The two-step mechanics of paragraph 46(a) match Illustrative Example 17: VERIFIED (web, secondary reproduction of IE17; ifrs.org IE listing).</p></li><li><p>IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision on substitution rights, IFRIC Update November 2022 (a right exercisable only on malfunction or repair is not substantive). VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org IFRIC Update, via search summary; checker's confirmation).</p></li><li><p>KPMG, "IFRS 16 Leases: upstream oil and gas" (2017): scope exclusion for leases to explore for or use oil and gas, substitution rights for rigs, the USD 5,000 low-value benchmark in the basis for conclusions. VERIFIED (web, assets.kpmg.com).</p></li><li><p>IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision "Liabilities in relation to a Joint Operator's Interest in a Joint Operation (IFRS 11)", March 2019. The decision addressed the operator's liability and expressly left the asset side unaddressed. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org agenda decision and iasplus.com summary).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 111 <em>Pengaturan Bersama</em> (IFRS 11 <em>Joint Arrangements</em>). STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Amendment to PSAK 212 (formerly PSAK 46; IAS 12), deferred tax related to assets and liabilities arising from a single transaction, effective 1 January 2023. VERIFIED (web, IAI DSAK exposure draft "DE Amendemen PSAK 46", web.iaiglobal.or.id; checker's confirmation).</p></li><li><p>ISAK 101 (IFRIC 1 <em>Changes in Existing Decommissioning, Restoration and Similar Liabilities</em>). STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 107 <em>Instrumen Keuangan: Pengungkapan</em> (IFRS 7), paragraphs 39 and B11. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PP 79/2010 as amended by PP 27/2017, cost recovery and income tax in upstream oil and gas. Named from a web search result; text not opened. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Owner's study notes, Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan, summary at <code>_referensi/rumpun-akuntansi-keuangan/02-inter-ta.md</code>. Used for explanatory style only; they do not cover leases. NOTES.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A right-of-use asset is a lessee's right to use a leased asset, measured from the present value of the lease payments and depreciated straight-line. Whether a rig contract creates one depends on who directs the rig.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The line falls every year through depreciation. In the case below, a three-year and a five-year lease lose 45.7 percent of their carrying amount in their second year with no event at all.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A fall has to be split into depreciation, terminations and extensions using the asset rollforward, the lease liability and the maturity table. In the case, the three combine into a 39.0 percent fall.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The reported line fell 39 percent, and a fall that size can come from depreciation alone","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The balance sheet on this page reports right-of-use assets, net, of 123,977 at 31 December 2024 against 203,379 a year earlier, in thousands. That is a fall of 79,402, or 39.0 percent, taking the line from 0.65 to 0.41 percent of total assets.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"This essay uses PT Hulu Energi Nusantara (\"HEN\"), an upstream oil and gas contractor operating under production sharing contracts (PSCs) with SKK Migas, reporting in USD under Indonesian SAK with a 31 December year end. HEN and every figure attached to it are constructed for illustration. HEN amounts are in USD thousand; day rates are in USD thousand per day.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN operates its block with a 60 percent participating interest, its share of the block's costs and production; partners hold the other 40 percent, and the PSC runs to 2040 (constructed). On 1 January 2023 HEN leased a jack-up rig (an offshore drilling rig that stands on legs lowered to the seabed) for three years and an onshore warehouse for five. In February 2025 the audit committee chair, reading the draft 2024 notes, sees right-of-use assets down from 37,801.0 to 23,051.7, a fall of 39.0 percent, and asks whether the rig was released early or the leases are running down.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The governing claim of this essay is that a change in right-of-use assets nets depreciation, which runs every year, against term events, which only the lease liability and the maturity table (the note listing undiscounted lease payments by due date) reveal, so the percentage alone cannot say which happened.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"This is a mechanism essay. Its verdict is that HEN's 39.0 percent fall nets a 45.7 percent fall from depreciation, a rig release and a warehouse extension, a split any reader can rebuild from the notes.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The governing standard is PSAK 116 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Leases","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 73, IFRS 16), effective in Indonesia since 2020 under its old number. It replaced PSAK 30 (IAS 17), which kept operating leases off the lessee's balance sheet.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A rig contract creates a right-of-use asset only if HEN directs the rig","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A contract contains a lease when it conveys the right to control the use of an identified asset for a period in exchange for consideration (PSAK 116 paragraph 9), which means the right to substantially all of its economic benefits and the right to direct how and for what purpose it is used. Otherwise it is a service, and nothing reaches this line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN's contract names one jack-up, and the owner may substitute another only if that rig is damaged. A right that applies only when the asset is not working does not stop it being identified (paragraph B18). A general right would also fail, because a substitution right counts only if the supplier could practically substitute and would gain from doing so (paragraph B14), and moving a jack-up costs its owner several million USD (in whole dollars). The asset is identified.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN decides which wells the rig drills, in what order, to what depth and when it moves. Those are the decisions about how and for what purpose the rig is used (paragraph B24). The owner's crew operates the rig on HEN's instructions, which gives the owner no such right. HEN has a lease. Had a drilling company undertaken to drill five wells with any rig from its fleet, choosing which rig and when, HEN would be buying a service.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The exclusion of leases to explore for or use oil and gas (paragraph 3(a)) covers the PSC's right to the resource, not the rig or the warehouse.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The day rate also pays for services. HEN pays USD 80 thousand a day for the rig, crew, maintenance and catering. Rigs of this class charter bareboat, the rig alone with the charterer supplying the crew, for about USD 48 thousand a day, and the services are priced at USD 32 thousand. PSAK 116 allocates the price on relative stand-alone prices (paragraph 13).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lease component = 80 × 48 / (48 + 32) = USD 48 thousand per day","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Annual lease payment = 48 × 365 = 17,520; annual service cost = 32 × 365 = 11,680, recognised as the services are received","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A lessee may elect, by class of asset, not to separate the components (paragraph 15). The liability would then capitalise 29,200 a year, 76,630.0 instead of the 45,978.0 computed below, 67 percent more.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The liability is the present value of the payments, and the asset adds what HEN spent to get the assets into use","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The lease liability at commencement is the present value of the unpaid lease payments, discounted at the rate implicit in the lease or, if that cannot be readily determined, at the lessee's incremental borrowing rate, what it would pay to borrow over a similar term against similar security (paragraph 26). HEN uses 7 percent. Both leases pay annually in arrears, simplifying monthly invoicing, and the annuity factor (the present value of 1 a year for n years) is (1 − 1.07^−n) / 0.07.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rig liability = 17,520 × (1 − 1.07^−3) / 0.07 = 17,520 × 2.624316 = 45,978.0","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Warehouse liability = 1,925 × (1 − 1.07^−5) / 0.07 = 1,925 × 4.100197 = 7,892.9","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The right-of-use asset is the liability plus lease payments made at or before commencement, initial direct costs and the estimated cost of restoring the site that the lease obliges the lessee to bear (paragraph 24). HEN paid the rig owner a mobilisation fee of 1,000 in December 2022 to tow the rig to location. HEN treats it as a payment for making the leased rig available and puts it all in the lease component; a 48/32 allocation would put 600 there. HEN paid an agent's commission of 60 on the warehouse, and must restore the floor slab at exit at an estimated 210.4 in 2027, 150.0 discounted at 7 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rig asset = 45,978.0 + 1,000 = 46,978.0","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Warehouse asset = 7,892.9 + 60 + 150.0 = 8,102.9","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Right-of-use assets 55,080.9","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"Cr Lease liabilities 53,870.9","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"Cr Prepaid mobilisation 1,000.0","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"Cr Cash (agent's commission) 60.0","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"Cr Restoration provision 150.0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Straight-line depreciation plus effective interest front-loads the cost of a lease","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The rig costs HEN 18,877.8 in its first year against a payment of 17,520, because interest is charged on the opening liability at the discount rate (the effective interest method) and is largest when the liability is largest, while depreciation is straight-line over the shorter of the lease term and the asset's life.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Opening liability","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest at 7%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Payment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Closing liability","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depreciation","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest plus depreciation","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"45,978.0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,218.5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"17,520","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31,676.5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15,659.3","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"18,877.8","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31,676.5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,217.4","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"17,520","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"16,373.8","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15,659.3","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"17,876.7","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"16,373.8","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,146.2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"17,520","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0.0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15,659.4","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"16,805.6","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depreciation = 46,978.0 / 3 = 15,659.3 a year, with 0.1 of rounding in 2025","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total charge = 46,978.0 + 3,218.5 + 2,217.4 + 1,146.2 = 53,560.1, the 52,560 of payments plus the 1,000 fee, with 0.1 of rounding","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under PSAK 30 the rig would have cost 17,520 + 1,000 / 3 = 17,853.3 a year; PSAK 116 charges 1,024.5 more in 2023 and 1,047.7 less in 2025. Where the rig drills development wells, the depreciation and the services are capitalised into oil and gas properties and reach profit through depletion.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The warehouse depreciates at 8,102.9 / 5 = 1,620.6 a year. At 31 December 2023 the line holds 31,318.7 for the rig and 6,482.3 for the warehouse, 37,801.0 in total.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The asset is non-current in full. The current/non-current boundary runs through the liability, whose current part is the principal due within twelve months. At 31 December 2023 that is 15,302.7 for the rig (31,676.5 − 16,373.8) and 1,468.6 for the warehouse (6,520.4 − 5,051.8), 16,771.3 in total, leaving 38,196.9 − 16,771.3 = 21,425.6 non-current.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Short-term and low-value leases never reach the line","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A lessee may expense two kinds of lease straight-line instead (paragraph 5). A short-term lease runs 12 months or less from commencement with no purchase option, elected by class of asset. A low-value lease, judged lease by lease, is one whose asset is worth about USD 5,000 (in whole dollars) or less when new, such as a laptop. In 2024 HEN expensed a 75-day liftboat (a self-elevating service vessel) hire at USD 12 thousand a day, 900 in total.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The lease term is a judgment, and it moves the line in both directions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Two board decisions on 31 December 2024 moved HEN's leases in opposite directions. The lease term is the non-cancellable period plus option periods the lessee is reasonably certain, a threshold well above \"more likely than not\", to extend into or not to terminate. The warehouse carried a ten-year extension option at the same rent. At commencement HEN had not decided where to hold production spares after drilling, and a shared base with a neighbouring operator was an option, so the extension was not reasonably certain.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The term is reassessed when a significant event within the lessee's control changes that certainty (paragraph 20), such as a business decision or significant leasehold improvements (paragraph B41). In 2024 an appraisal well, drilled to measure a discovery, came up dry. That ended drilling, but it was outside HEN's control and says nothing about storage. On 31 December 2024 the board approved a production plan that makes the warehouse the field's spares hub until 2037, within the PSC's term. By that date HEN had bolted 1,800 of heavy racking to the slab; leaving early would forfeit most of it and cost about 250 to remove. The decision and the racking are within HEN's control, as paragraph 20 requires, and make the extension reasonably certain. The liability is remeasured over thirteen remaining payments at the incremental borrowing rate at that date, 8 percent for the tenor, and the asset moves by the same amount (paragraphs 39–40).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revised warehouse liability = 1,925 × (1 − 1.08^−13) / 0.08 = 1,925 × 7.903776 = 15,214.8","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Increase = 15,214.8 − 5,051.8 = 10,163.0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Right-of-use assets 10,163.0 / Cr Lease liabilities 10,163.0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After two years of unwinding at 7 percent, charged as finance cost, the restoration provision stood at 210.4 / 1.07^3 = 171.7; due in 2037 it is 210.4 / 1.07^13 = 87.3, and under ISAK 101 (IFRIC 1) the 84.4 change reduces the asset.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Restoration provision 84.4 / Cr Right-of-use assets 84.4","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The board's other decision cut the drilling programme after the dry well. The rig contract had no early-termination option, but on 31 December 2024 the owner agreed to an early release (a partial termination) on 30 June 2025, six months early and without a fee, because another client was waiting. A change agreed after commencement and outside the original terms is a lease modification, and one that decreases scope is accounted for in two steps (paragraph 46(a)). First, asset and liability are cut in proportion to the scope removed, the difference going to profit or loss. Second, the liability is remeasured at a revised rate (HEN's six-month rate, 7 percent), and the asset moves by that amount. HEN measures scope by remaining term, a judgment, so it removes half.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rig asset before the modification = 31,318.7 − 15,659.3 = 15,659.4; rig liability = 16,373.8","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Step 1: asset reduced by 15,659.4 × 50% = 7,829.7; liability reduced by 16,373.8 × 50% = 8,186.9","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gain on modification = 8,186.9 − 7,829.7 = 357.2","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Step 2: revised liability = 8,760 due 30 June 2025 / 1.07^0.5 = 8,468.6; adjustment = 8,468.6 − (16,373.8 − 8,186.9) = 281.7","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Lease liabilities 8,186.9 / Cr Right-of-use assets 7,829.7 / Cr Gain on lease modification 357.2","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Right-of-use assets 281.7 / Cr Lease liabilities 281.7","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The rig's carrying amount at 31 December 2024 is 15,659.4 − 7,829.7 + 281.7 = 8,111.4. Had a termination right been in the original contract, becoming reasonably certain to use it would have been a reassessment, with the whole change taken to the asset and no gain.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The rollforward, the liability and the maturity table separate depreciation from term events","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN's line fell 14,749.3 in 2024, less than its depreciation of 17,279.9, because the term events added a net 2,530.6.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rig","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Warehouse","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 January","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31,318.7","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,482.3","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"37,801.0","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depreciation","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(15,659.3)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(1,620.6)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(17,279.9)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Modification, step 1","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(7,829.7)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(7,829.7)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Modification, step 2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"281.7","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"281.7","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Extension reassessment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10,163.0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10,163.0","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Restoration estimate","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(84.4)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(84.4)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8,111.4","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"14,940.3","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"23,051.7","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depreciation alone = 37,801.0 − 17,279.9 = 20,521.1, a fall of 45.7 percent","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Adding the rig modification = 20,521.1 − 7,829.7 + 281.7 = 12,973.1, a fall of 65.7 percent","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Adding the warehouse events = 12,973.1 + 10,163.0 − 84.4 = 23,051.7, a fall of 39.0 percent","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The liability gives a second test, because with no term events it falls by the principal repaid. HEN paid 19,445 in 2024 against interest of 2,217.4 + 456.4 = 2,673.8, repaying 16,771.2. The liability fell from 38,196.9 to 23,683.4 (8,468.6 for the rig and 15,214.8 for the warehouse), only 14,513.5. The gap is the rig modification, 8,186.9 − 281.7 = 7,905.2 down, and the extension, 10,163.0 up (0.1 of rounding). A liability that falls more than principal repaid signals terminations; less signals new leases or extensions.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The maturity table, which PSAK 116 paragraph 58 requires through PSAK 107 (formerly 60, IFRS 7) paragraphs 39 and B11, places the event in time.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Undiscounted lease payments","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Within 1 year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"19,445","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10,685","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 to 2 years","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"19,445","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,925","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2 to 5 years","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,850","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5,775","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Over 5 years","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15,400","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"42,740","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"33,785","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Last year's \"1 to 2 years\" amount of 19,445 should reappear as this year's \"within 1 year\". It shows 10,685, so 8,760, six months of rig payments, has gone, a termination. The \"over 5 years\" amount appears from nothing, an extension or a new long lease. The total is 33,785 against 42,740 − 19,445 = 23,295 with no events, the difference being 10 × 1,925 = 19,250 added less 8,760 removed. The same tests would say whether the reported 39 percent is runoff without renewal or leases ended early.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At a PSC operator the line carries the partners' share, and cost recovery runs on a different clock","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN carries 100 percent of both leases on its balance sheet although it bears only 60 percent of their cost, because it signed them in its own name as operator. In March 2019 the IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded that under IFRS 11, now PSAK 111 (formerly 66), a joint operator recognises the liabilities for which it has primary responsibility, including a lease on which it is the primary obligor, the party the lessor can pursue for payment. The partners repay 40 percent of each payment as partner reimbursements billed under the joint operating agreement (JOA), so 40% × 23,051.7 = 9,220.7 of the line is theirs in substance.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The asset side is less settled, and the Committee left it unaddressed. If the JOA gives the partners a right to use the rig, HEN has a sublease, a lease of the asset onward to them, and moves their share of the asset into a receivable. If HEN alone directs the rig, it keeps the full asset, which is HEN's policy. It then books partner reimbursements as they are billed, in 2024 Dr Receivable from partners 7,778 (40% × 19,445) / Cr Lease costs 7,778. Reimbursements follow the payments while the expense follows depreciation and interest, so HEN's net charge is front-loaded. Non-operating partners book their share through the billings.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost recovery is the PSC mechanism by which the contractor recoups approved operating costs from production before the remainder is split with the government. This essay assumes rentals are recovered as the lease payments fall due, not as depreciation and interest, and that the rig, unlike equipment HEN buys, stays the owner's property. If PSC tax deductions follow the same payments, the asset, the lease liability and (if restoration is deductible when spent) the provision all have a tax base, the amount the tax rules attribute to them, of nil. Since the PSAK 212 (formerly 46, IAS 12) single-transaction amendment, effective 1 January 2023, these differences are recognised gross. On HEN's 60 percent share at the combined PSC rate of 40 percent, that is a deferred tax liability of 5,532.4 on the asset against deferred tax assets of 5,684.0 on the lease liability and 21.0 on the provision, a net asset of 172.6 at 31 December 2024.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN's line fell 39.0 percent because a warehouse extension offset most of a rig release in a year when depreciation alone would have cut 45.7 percent","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The chair's answer is that the rig was released early, the leases ran down, and a third event pulled the other way. Depreciation alone would have left 20,521.1; the rig release removed a net 7,548.0 (7,829.7 less 281.7) with a gain of 357.2; the warehouse extension added a net 10,078.6 (10,163.0 less 84.4). Without the rollforward, the modification gain and the maturity table in the notes, HEN's 39.0 percent reads exactly like the reported company's.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost recovery and PSC tax following lease payments and restoration spend, and the rig remaining the lessor's property, were not checked against a PSC, PP 79/2010 as amended or SKK Migas rules. If deductions followed the accounting charge, the deferred tax would be nil.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The asset-side treatment in the joint operation (no sublease, partner reimbursements credited to lease costs) is a constructed policy. Deferred tax on the partners' 40 percent carried gross is left out.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Putting the whole mobilisation fee in the lease component is a judgment; an auditor could require the 48/32 allocation, which would make the rig asset 46,578.0.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Measuring a term reduction by remaining term in step 1 is one reading; the standard's illustrative example reduces floor space, not term.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The PSAK 116 paragraph numbers follow IFRS 16 and were not checked against the Indonesian text; real rig contracts add standby and moving rates that complicate the 48/32 allocation.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 116 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Sewa","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 73; IFRS 16 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Leases","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), paragraphs 3, 5, 9, 13, 15, 20, 24, 26, 39–40, 46, 58, B14, B18, B24, B41. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. The two-step mechanics of paragraph 46(a) match Illustrative Example 17: VERIFIED (web, secondary reproduction of IE17; ifrs.org IE listing).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision on substitution rights, IFRIC Update November 2022 (a right exercisable only on malfunction or repair is not substantive). VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org IFRIC Update, via search summary; checker's confirmation).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"KPMG, \"IFRS 16 Leases: upstream oil and gas\" (2017): scope exclusion for leases to explore for or use oil and gas, substitution rights for rigs, the USD 5,000 low-value benchmark in the basis for conclusions. VERIFIED (web, assets.kpmg.com).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision \"Liabilities in relation to a Joint Operator's Interest in a Joint Operation (IFRS 11)\", March 2019. The decision addressed the operator's liability and expressly left the asset side unaddressed. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org agenda decision and iasplus.com summary).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 111 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Pengaturan Bersama","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 11 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Joint Arrangements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"). STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Amendment to PSAK 212 (formerly PSAK 46; IAS 12), deferred tax related to assets and liabilities arising from a single transaction, effective 1 January 2023. VERIFIED (web, IAI DSAK exposure draft \"DE Amendemen PSAK 46\", web.iaiglobal.or.id; checker's confirmation).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ISAK 101 (IFRIC 1 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Changes in Existing Decommissioning, Restoration and Similar Liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"). STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 107 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Instrumen Keuangan: Pengungkapan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 7), paragraphs 39 and B11. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PP 79/2010 as amended by PP 27/2017, cost recovery and income tax in upstream oil and gas. Named from a web search result; text not opened. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Owner's study notes, Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan, summary at ","type":"text"},{"text":"_referensi/rumpun-akuntansi-keuangan/02-inter-ta.md","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":". Used for explanatory style only; they do not cover leases. NOTES.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"Lease or service, measurement, term judgments and the operator's gross-up at a PSC contractor, and how the lease liability and maturity table separate runoff from termination","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["A right-of-use asset is a lessee's right to use a leased asset, measured from the present value of the lease payments and depreciated straight-line. Whether a rig contract creates one depends on who directs the rig.","The line falls every year through depreciation. In the case below, a three-year and a five-year lease lose 45.7 percent of their carrying amount in their second year with no event at all.","A fall has to be split into depreciation, terminations and extensions using the asset rollforward, the lease liability and the maturity table. In the case, the three combine into a 39.0 percent fall."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = 'a295ed2d-9b80-47b0-88a6-b992b49c21b9';
